<Post title="Guaranteed delivery: Lean verification" description="Exact theorem domains, remaining proof obligations and reproducible Lean sources." updated={release.publication_date}>
  <p><a href="/cop-prisoner/">Exact-count overview</a> · <a href="/cop-prisoner/proofs/">Classification proofs</a> · <a href="/cop-prisoner/paper/">Paper and evidence</a> · <a href="/cop-prisoner/delivery-time/">Delivery-time companion</a></p>
  <p><strong>Full Lean verification is in progress.</strong> The ordinary classification gives the exact maximum and an explicit attaining graph for every admissible pair. The formal proofs below establish substantial complete domains, but do not yet assemble that full classification.</p>
  <p>The downloadable snapshot uses <strong>Lean 4.33.1 with Std only</strong>. All {verification.modules.length} distributed modules passed the recorded replay. The <a href="/cop-prisoner/lean-verification.json">receipt</a> identifies their source hashes, compiler, outputs and logical axioms. The module count is an inventory, not a percentage of completeness.</p>
  <h2>Exact maxima and explicit constructions already verified</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>The complete dense range for every <i>n ≥ 20</i>.</strong> For <i>n(n − 3) ≤ m ≤ n(n − 1)</i>, Lean proves the maximum and a concrete attaining graph. At <i>m = n(n − 3)</i> the value is <i>2n</i>; for <i>r = m − n(n − 3)</i> with <i>1 ≤ r ≤ n</i>, it is <i>2n − r − ⌈r/2⌉ − 1</i>; above <i>n(n − 2)</i> it is zero. The arbitrary-graph upper bound, root and core constructions, exact arrow counts and attaining scores are all proved. <a href="/cop-prisoner/lean/DenseClassification.lean">DenseClassification</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>The adjacent band for every <i>n ≥ 16</i>.</strong> For <i>n(n − 4) ≤ m ≤ n(n − 3)</i>, the maximum equals the number of missing pairs, <i>n(n − 1) − m</i>. Explicit even and odd formulas and finite repairs cover every order in this domain. Every optional subset works within two moves; taking the first required optional arrows gives the exact budget. Together with the preceding result, this verifies every budget <i>m ≥ n(n − 4)</i> when <i>n ≥ 20</i>. <a href="/cop-prisoner/lean/DenseEnvelopeClassification.lean">DenseEnvelopeClassification</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>The functional endpoint at every <i>n ≥ 2</i>.</strong> The exact value at <i>m = n(n − 2)</i> is <i>⌊(n − 2)/2⌋</i>. The proof characterizes all successful missing pairs in complements of fixed-point-free functions and supplies a literal paired-root attainer. <a href="/cop-prisoner/lean/FunctionalEndpoint.lean">FunctionalEndpoint</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>The zero range at every order.</strong> More than <i>n(n − 2)</i> arrows force zero indirect guarantees. The first <i>m</i> nonloop ordered pairs give a counted attainer at every admissible budget in that range. <a href="/cop-prisoner/lean/DenseZero.lean">DenseZero</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>The twenty-four-arrow maximum on every host from twelve.</strong> Lean proves <i>M(n,24) = 108</i> for all <i>n ≥ 12</i>, combining a checked twelve-vertex witness, the general sparse upper bound and exact isolate padding. <a href="/cop-prisoner/lean/SparseAttainment.lean">SparseAttainment</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Prescribed five-offset circles.</strong> When <i>n ≥ 16</i> is divisible by four, every budget <i>5n ≤ m ≤ n(n/2 − 1)</i> attains all missing pairs. The parameter is prescribed from <i>n</i>; modular escape, coprime orbit coverage, actual delivery and exact optional-prefix counts are proved. A separate guarded family has a closed arithmetic endpoint under its displayed parameter conditions. <a href="/cop-prisoner/lean/CircleParameters.lean">CircleParameters</a> and <a href="/cop-prisoner/lean/CircleGuardedConstruction.lean">CircleGuardedConstruction</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Protected circles at every <i>n ≥ 25</i>.</strong> Every budget <i>3n ≤ m ≤ n(⌈n/7⌉ − 1)</i> attains all missing pairs. A proved arithmetic parameter selection and a specified greedy offset procedure supply the graph; Lean proves preservation of protected differences, the envelope size, delivery and exact budget. <a href="/cop-prisoner/lean/ProtectedParameters.lean">ProtectedParameters</a>.</li>
  </ol>
  <h2>General bounds and finite exclusions</h2>
  <p><a href="/cop-prisoner/lean/VertexBound.lean">VertexBound</a> proves <i>I(G) ≤ n(n − 3)</i> for every loopless graph with <i>n ≥ 3</i>. <a href="/cop-prisoner/lean/SparseParity.lean">SparseParity</a> proves the unrestricted budget bounds <i>I(G) ≤ k(k − 3)</i> for <i>2k</i> arrows and <i>I(G) ≤ k(k − 3) + 2</i> for <i>2k + 1</i> arrows, for every <i>k ≥ 5</i>. Support counting, deletion, strategy preservation and induction are discharged; the small auxiliary induction bounds are not the sharp finite table.</p>
  <p><a href="/cop-prisoner/lean/FiniteExclusionUnrestrictedNine.lean">The complete nine-vertex strictness proof</a> establishes <i>I(G) ≤ 53</i> for every loopless nine-vertex graph. It includes degree rigidity, matching factorization, canonical permutation cycles and all eight complete exclusion trees. This does not prove the sharper two-regular bound of 30 or all nine-vertex maxima. Further checked interfaces support optimistic partial-game pruning and symmetry reductions, whose concrete trees must still be replayed.</p>
  <p>Signed winning and losing certificates prove that the saved graphs at <i>(n,m) = (9,18), (10,20), (11,22)</i> have exact scores <i>30, 50, 79</i>. These are exact graph scores and hence lower witnesses for the extrema; their compilation alone does not prove an upper bound over all competing graphs. The <a href="/cop-prisoner/lean/SignedRankCertificate.lean">certificate kernel</a> checks both kinds of pair. The earlier four-vertex example and complete three-vertex exclusion pilot are retained.</p>
  <h2>The actual game and the trust boundary</h2>
  <p>Both players may wait; the messenger moves first and receipt precedes collision. <a href="/cop-prisoner/lean/SemanticCompleteness.lean">SemanticCompleteness</a> proves equivalence between finite winning trees and one fixed messenger policy that eventually delivers against every permitted initial cop and every legal history-dependent cop policy. No time bound is assumed in the actual-play definition.</p>
  <p><a href="/cop-prisoner/lean/GraphPadding.lean">Padding</a> preserves scores exactly when adding isolates or sinks with at most one predecessor, including cops starting at or entering the new sink. <a href="/cop-prisoner/lean/LocalArena.lean">LocalArena</a> proves projection and sequential composition for separate messenger/cop state spaces. A safe transfer includes the cop reply before the next stage; only terminal receipt waives collision at the recipient. Concrete geometry and certificate premises remain visible until instantiated.</p>
  <p>All finite checks use Lean kernel reduction. No external solver assertion, custom axiom, proof hole or native proof is accepted. Reported axioms are confined to Lean's standard <code>propext</code>, <code>Classical.choice</code> and <code>Quot.sound</code>. Certificate producers are untrusted and unnecessary for replaying the literal distributed sources.</p>
  <h2>What remains to finish</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>The sparse infinite attaining families, concrete local projections and full finite bridges.</li>
    <li>The remaining main-interval construction chain, bounded stages and complete parameter overlap.</li>
    <li>The sharp finite upper exclusions and complete small-order tables.</li>
    <li>The lower dense orders: the ordinary sharp threshold is eleven, while the uniform formal theorem above starts at twenty.</li>
    <li>One quantified theorem and explicit constructor covering every admissible <i>(n,m)</i>, with all these dependencies discharged.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Download the <a href="/cop-prisoner/lean-proofs.zip">Lean sources</a>, install the pinned toolchain and run <code>python3 check.py</code> inside the extracted <code>lean</code> folder. It rebuilds every distributed module sequentially with two workers and rejects source changes during replay. The <a href="/cop-prisoner/lean/README.md">theorem and replay guide</a> gives precise statements. The research archive contains the dependency-ordered roadmap; the <a href="/cop-prisoner/paper/#evidence-guide">evidence guide</a> separately describes the ordinary and external computational proofs.</p>
</Post>
